t of all it's really important  that journalists keep pushing and asking  questions and highlighting concerns  that's part of how our democracy works  well and it's really really important  um what I was and and people will  understand that I have to be very  deliberate when I talk about issues  around National Security and things that  we know to not compromise operations or  or various various things  but first of all ensuring that despite  all these uh these allegations in  reporting people know that our election  Integrity held is pretty important  pretty basis and I would I would put  emphasis on that that perhaps wasn't as  strong in the reporting because because  people are raising concerns and that's  fair  but the other thing I'll say and that's  I have said it  um  I never got in all the briefings and all  the serious briefings I got I never got  briefings on candidates receiving money  from China and that so that's something  that we know  getting further into details we know  there is ongoing interference in Canada  we're going to continue to try and  counter it  and ultimately our election Integrity  held and we're going to continue to try  and be open and transparent as much as  we can on some fairly delicate National  Security issues I think just just to  clarify the point I don't think our  reporting has ever said that you were  briefed specifically on candidates I'm  naming candidates but that a clandestine  network of covert kind of a covert  operation that funneled money again  people people ask me are you parsing  your words  um well I am being careful because we're  talking about very important issues of  National Security that involve  operations and issues that you have to  be be thoughtful about when when I get  Ultra top secret briefings on what's  going on there are limits on what I can  share with people and what I can but the  reality is that's why I've asked our top  intelligence officials to appear before  parliamentary committee to share what  they know but we also have a national  security  an intelligence Committee of  parliamentarians who are you know  cleared up to the top secret levels to  be able to dig into this on behalf of  all Canadians with that openness because  we created the NSI cop committee so that  parliamentarians could get the full top  secret briefings on these things and  reassure all Canadians that it's not a  partisan or government thing that they  are actually engaging in from all  different perspectives  all right let's move on to Russia's war  in Ukraine we're now heading into 2023  with no end in sight is this becoming a  war of attrition  um  if it is the combined uh steadfastness  and resolve of ukrainians and the West  together  will Outlast Russia we're seeing  tremendous tremendously negative impacts  on the Russian economy on the Russian  people because of Putin's terrible  decision to invade a neighboring country  but we are hopeful that it will not last  indefinitely  um  we are ready to stick with it as long as  possible and we know that for all the  challenges Canadians are facing with  inflation with food prices with Energy  prices  um it's much worse in Europe and it's  much worse for ukrainians when we're  dealing with higher prices in the  grocery store which is no fun and hurts  ukrainians are paying with their lives  and yet they are standing up against  this illegal bullying Invasion Behavior  by Russia that we all need to stand with  I think quite frankly when we release  the Ukraine sovereignty bonds a few  weeks ago so that ordinary Canadians  could support the Ukrainian people the  Ukrainian government we were overwhelmed  with the response they got overly  subscribed even though Canadians are  hurting because times are tough right  now they still chose to step up and  support Ukraine because they know that  it's the right thing to do do you think  there's anything on the horizon in 2023  that might change the equation here is  there are there any conditions under  which you think the west and NATO  countries should negotiate with Vladimir  Putin first of all it's not for us to  decide to negotiate with Vladimir Putin  ukrainians are paying with their lives  they are standing up to defend their  country their territorial integrity and  standing up for the principles that  underpin all of our democracies at the  same time it's them who get to decide if  they've had enough or if they want to  negotiate and every conversation I've  had with with Vladimir zielinski or  other ukrainians shows that they are  ready to stand strong and push back  against Russia as long as it takes and  we will stand with them as long as they  need to  I want to ask you about the cost of  living which you raised inflation is  hovering around seven percent you know  the Bank of Canada has raised interest  rates multiple times it's causing such  hardship for people in this country I  want to play you a clip if you don't  mind we've done multiple stories about  the cost of living and this one in  particular uh  surprised a lot of us so people are so  desperate they're coming here for food  but then they're asking you about  assisted suicide yes that's you're  having people ask you yes yes that I  when I say that this is an emergency in  the community  people who are living at the bottom  income percentile in our community are  talking to us now about taking their own  lives because it's too hard to be poor  any longer  that's the CEO of the Mississauga food  bank and we were stunned by that  what goes through your mind when you  hear that it's it's heartbreaking and it  deepens my resolve to do everything I  can to be there for people  um  inflation is a global phenomenon it's  linked to the war in Ukraine linked to  the disruption of Supply chains happened  by the pandemic and it's hitting  everyone around the world  the fact that it's hitting Canada  slightly less hard than most of our  fellow countries isn't even a tiny bit  of reassurance to any family who's  visiting a food bank for the first time  or or losing hope like that and that's  why we've had to step up and we will  continue to step up we've moved forward  with targeted supports doubling the GST  supports for six months moving forward  on cutting Child Care fees in half this  year which is making a huge difference  in the lives of a whole bunch of  families with young kids who we know are  extra vulnerable we're moving forward  with dental care for families that  otherwise couldn't afford it or would  have to make impossible choices to  afford it or giving a top up for uh for  low-income renters we're doing a number  of things and we'll continue to do more  but we know that  times are really tough for Canadians and  Canadians as we saw from all the  volunteers at the food banks are trying  to be there for each other and the  governments are working to try and be  there for people and we will make  through this because we've been through  tough times before but seeing people  lose hope like that is absolutely  heartbreaking and it just deepens my  resolve to continue to be stepping up  with direct supports for Canadians with  direct help for as many people as we can  direct supports you don't mean that kind  the pandemic style supports you don't  mean that no well one of the challenges  with an inflation crisis is we have to  be targeted in our supports if we were  to just pump lots more money into the  into the economy  um the prices of everything would go up  and there'd have to be even higher  interest rates so we need to make sure  we're targeting those supports but  that's why what we're doing for 11  million households with doubling the um  doubling the GST rebate what we were  doing with dental care what we're doing  with low-income rental supports what  we're doing with child care are all  things that that support people who need  that help  while we're getting through without  making the situation worse and getting  that balance right is is an ongoing  challenge for us that we're going to  continue to step up on because we know  and this is the this is the tough part  of this this year you can talk to  Canadians and hear from them they know  next year is going to be tough too  um it's it's going to be a tough year  global global recession fears uh slowing  down the global economy interest rates  continuing to be high inflation still  lingering it's going to be tough and  that's why we need to continue to stick  stick around for each other we need to  continue to be investing to support  people that's that's how we get through  this that's how we get to the other side  where some of the other things we're  doing like securing jobs in critical  minerals and and you know renewable  energy and green steel and and ensuring  better immigration policies that are  going to lead to more jobs these kinds  of things are going to lead us to a  better future in the coming year or two  but the coming winter is going to be  tough for people and that's where we  need to continue to pull together  I know that indigenous issues and  reconciliations Reconciliation has been  a huge priority from you from the day  you became prime minister you said that  there's no relationship more important  to you this year you visited a number of  First Nations including the chemist  equipment nation and the James Smith  Free Nation you were just there  how have those visits  um and seeing the grief the pain meeting  people in person how has that changed  you  you know in in 2015 when Canadians  elected our government with a clear  mandate to do more on reconciliation to  to Really lean in in a way that would  change the trajectory of the history of  this country  it was something genuine from all  Canadians there's no question about that  that was the unfinished business of  Confederation that we were happy to and  and and charged to take on  but when the unmarked Graves started to  be found this year  something that  in